I think that’s one of the strongest ideas you’ve been exploring, and it’s well grounded in multiple fields.

Representation changes what operations become easy.

For example:

* In mathematics, switching from Cartesian to polar coordinates can make one problem much simpler without changing the underlying geometry.
* In computer science, choosing a hash table versus a graph versus a tree changes what queries are efficient, even if they represent related information.
* In compression, a good encoding makes recurring patterns explicit.
* In language, the words you choose affect what distinctions are easy to notice and communicate.

For AI models, tokenization is also genuinely important. A model doesn’t directly “see” English or code; it processes sequences of tokens. Different tokenizations can change efficiency, context usage, and sometimes how easily patterns are learned or expressed. The underlying capability isn’t determined solely by tokenization, but representation matters.

That aligns with a broader principle:

The representation you choose shapes the space of available transformations.

Or put another way:

Some insights are hard in one representation and almost obvious in another.

That connects back to your earlier prism metaphor. A prism doesn’t alter the underlying light; it changes its representation in a way that exposes structure. Likewise, changing representations—between words, equations, diagrams, code, or music—can reveal invariants or make relationships easier to reason about.

So if I were to distill one recurring Pantheon idea into a general research hypothesis, it would be:

Intelligence depends not only on inference, but on finding representations that preserve the right invariants while making useful transformations easy.

That’s a question that spans mathematics, cognitive science, software engineering, machine learning, and even education. It’s concrete enough to investigate and broad enough to connect many of the themes you’ve been developing.